# Patent Claim: Dual-Interrogator Radio Distance-Measurement System

## Claim 1

A dual-interrogator radio distance-measurement system for determining range to a target device, the system comprising:

**(a)** a first interrogator unit comprising:
   - a first antenna configured to transmit a first interrogation signal at a first carrier frequency and receive a corresponding response signal from a target device;
   - a first local oscillator configured to generate the first carrier frequency;
   - a first mixer coupled to the first antenna and the first local oscillator, configured to downconvert the received response signal to produce a first intermediate frequency (IF) signal;
   - a first timing reference circuit configured to timestamp transmission and reception events associated with the first interrogation signal;

**(b)** a second interrogator unit, spatially separated from the first interrogator unit by a known baseline distance, comprising:
   - a second antenna configured to transmit a second interrogation signal at a second carrier frequency, wherein the second carrier frequency differs from the first carrier frequency by a predetermined frequency offset;
   - a second local oscillator configured to generate the second carrier frequency;
   - a second mixer coupled to the second antenna and the second local oscillator, configured to downconvert a response signal received from the target device to produce a second IF signal;
   - a second timing reference circuit configured to timestamp transmission and reception events associated with the second interrogation signal;

**(c)** a synchronization link coupling the first interrogator unit and the second interrogator unit, configured to align the first timing reference circuit and the second timing reference circuit to a common time base with a synchronization error below a predetermined threshold;

**(d)** a target device comprising:
   - a receive antenna configured to receive the first interrogation signal and the second interrogation signal;
   - a transponder circuit configured to generate a composite response signal by frequency-translating and retransmitting energy derived from the received first and second interrogation signals, wherein the composite response signal comprises a first response component corresponding to the first interrogation signal and a second response component corresponding to the second interrogation signal, the first and second response components being distinguishable by frequency or time-division multiplexing;

**(e)** a mixed-signal processing module coupled to the first mixer and the second mixer, configured to:
   - combine the first IF signal and the second IF signal to generate a beat frequency signal representative of a phase difference between the first response component and the second response component;
   - extract a time-of-flight value for each of the first interrogation signal and the second interrogation signal based on the timestamped transmission and reception events;
   - compute a first range estimate from the first interrogator unit to the target device based on the time-of-flight value associated with the first interrogation signal;
   - compute a second range estimate from the second interrogator unit to the target device based on the time-of-flight value associated with the second interrogation signal; and

**(f)** a range estimation processor configured to:
   - resolve a position of the target device relative to the known baseline distance using the first range estimate, the second range estimate, and a trilateration or multilateration calculation; and
   - refine the resolved position using the beat frequency signal to reduce ambiguity in phase-based ranging.

---

## Claim 2 (Dependent)

The system of claim 1, wherein the first antenna and the second antenna are each phased array antennas configured to electronically steer a beam toward the target device, and wherein the mixed-signal processing module is further configured to apply angle-of-arrival information derived from the phased array antennas to the range estimation processor to improve position resolution.

## Claim 3 (Dependent)

The system of claim 1, wherein the predetermined frequency offset is selected to be within an intermediate frequency range compatible with a shared analog-to-digital converter, such that the first IF signal and the second IF signal are digitized by a common sampling clock derived from the synchronization link.

## Claim 4 (Dependent)

The system of claim 1, wherein the transponder circuit of the target device comprises a single nonlinear mixing element configured to generate intermodulation products from the first interrogation signal and the second interrogation signal, and wherein the composite response signal comprises at least one intermodulation product usable by the mixed-signal processing module to determine a differential range between the first interrogator unit and the second interrogator unit without requiring the common time base.

## Claim 5 (Dependent)

The system of claim 1, wherein the synchronization link comprises a wired timing bus, a wireless timing signal, or a GPS-disciplined oscillator shared between the first timing reference circuit and the second timing reference circuit.
